[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should user preferences be stored? → Browser localStorage (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m localStorage it is.

  Second question: [1mwhat actually goes in it, and does this slice include UI?[0m Right now there are no settings in the app
  at all — no theme, no toggles, nothing — so "preferences" has no concrete referent yet. The options differ in how much
  of the work is done when we stop:

  - [1mStorage module only[0m — a [38;5;153mpreferences[39m module with [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m/[38;5;153mdefaults[39m/[38;5;153msubscribe[39m, plus tests. No UI, no settings defined;
    the app doesn't visibly change. Smallest honest slice, and everything after it is additive.
  - [1mModule + one real setting wired end-to-end[0m — the same module, plus one concrete preference actually read and written
    by the existing page (a remembered username in the login field is the natural fit, since that's the only UI there
    is). Proves the thing works in the real app rather than only in tests. Slightly bigger.
  - [1mModule + a settings panel[0m — adds a new settings UI section to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m with a couple of controls (theme,
    remember-me). Most visible, but it means designing UI for an app that currently has one form.

  I recommend the middle one: the module plus a remembered-username preference wired into the existing login form. It
  gives you a tested, swappable storage layer [3mand[0m proves it persists across a real reload, without inventing a settings
  screen nobody asked for. One flag on it — a remembered username in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable by any script on the page
  and by anyone with the device; that's normal for "remember me," but it's a deliberate choice, and passwords stay out
  of storage regardless.
